    "rich_text_9mtTfK": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "container_hmfBd7": {
          "type": "container",
          "name": "t:blocks.container.name",
          "settings": {
            "background_color": "#e8f2ff",
            "border_radius": 12,
            "border_width": 0,
            "border_color": "#e8f2ff",
            "border_top": true,
            "border_right": true,
            "border_bottom": true,
            "border_left": true,
            "padding_vertical": 52,
            "padding_horizontal": 52,
            "padding_vertical_mobile": 52,
            "padding_horizontal_mobile": 52
          },
          "blocks": {
            "heading_D8fypD": {
              "type": "heading",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "Hot flashes&nbsp;",
                "heading_size": "medium",
                "heading_style": "default",
                "heading_tag": "h3",
                "enable_link": false,
                "link": "",
                "open_new_tab": false,
                "highlight_option": "underline-hand",
                "word_animation_color": "#272727",
                "font_family": "heading",
                "alignment": "left",
                "text_color": "",
                "custom_font_size": false,
                "font_size": 52,
                "font_size_mobile": 40,
                "custom_line_height": false,
                "line_height": 60,
                "line_height_mobile": 48,
                "custom_spacing": true,
                "margin_top": 0,
                "margin_bottom": 10,
                "margin_left": 0,
                "margin_right": 0
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
            "text_ypNLRA": {
              "type": "text",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<p>Sudden waves of heat, flushing and sweating. The symptom women most associate with menopause, and one of the most manageable.</p>",
                "text_style": "body",
                "alignment": "left",
                "text_color": "",
                "custom_font_size": false,
                "font_size": 16,
                "font_size_mobile": 15,
                "custom_line_height": false,
                "line_height": 26,
                "line_height_mobile": 24,
                "custom_spacing": false,
                "margin_top": 0,
                "margin_bottom": 16,
                "margin_left": 0,
                "margin_right": 0
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
            "heading_C89a3w": {
              "type": "heading",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "What is happening&nbsp;",
                "heading_size": "small",
                "heading_style": "default",
                "heading_tag": "h3",
                "enable_link": false,
                "link": "",
                "open_new_tab": false,
                "highlight_option": "underline-hand",
                "word_animation_color": "#272727",
                "font_family": "heading",
                "alignment": "left",
                "text_color": "",
                "custom_font_size": true,
                "font_size": 26,
                "font_size_mobile": 40,
                "custom_line_height": false,
                "line_height": 60,
                "line_height_mobile": 48,
                "custom_spacing": true,
                "margin_top": 40,
                "margin_bottom": 20,
                "margin_left": 0,
                "margin_right": 0
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
            "text_MemLgz": {
              "type": "text",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<p>A hot flash is a sudden feeling of heat, usually in the face, neck and chest, often with flushing and sweating. Most last between one and five minutes. During perimenopause, changing estrogen levels affect the part of the brain that regulates body temperature, so its comfort zone narrows and small changes set off a heat response. Hot flashes that happen during sleep are called night sweats.</p><p>They are common. In a 2025 survey of more than 17,000 women, about seven in ten women over 35 reported hot flashes or night sweats. For most women they ease with time, but \"common\" does not mean you have to just live with them.</p>",
                "text_style": "body",
                "alignment": "left",
                "text_color": "",
                "custom_font_size": false,
                "font_size": 16,
                "font_size_mobile": 15,
                "custom_line_height": false,
                "line_height": 26,
                "line_height_mobile": 24,
                "custom_spacing": false,
                "margin_top": 0,
                "margin_bottom": 16,
                "margin_left": 0,
                "margin_right": 0
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
            "heading_yQBCqL": {
              "type": "heading",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "What may help&nbsp;",
                "heading_size": "small",
                "heading_style": "default",
                "heading_tag": "h3",
                "enable_link": false,
                "link": "",
                "open_new_tab": false,
                "highlight_option": "underline-hand",
                "word_animation_color": "#272727",
                "font_family": "heading",
                "alignment": "left",
                "text_color": "",
                "custom_font_size": true,
                "font_size": 26,
                "font_size_mobile": 40,
                "custom_line_height": false,
                "line_height": 60,
                "line_height_mobile": 48,
                "custom_spacing": true,
                "margin_top": 40,
                "margin_bottom": 20,
                "margin_left": 0,
                "margin_right": 0
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
            "text_CCB6zL": {
              "type": "text",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Everyday steps.</strong> Many women get real relief from simple changes:</p>",
                "text_style": "body",
                "alignment": "left",
                "text_color": "",
                "custom_font_size": false,
                "font_size": 16,
                "font_size_mobile": 15,
                "custom_line_height": false,
                "line_height": 26,
                "line_height_mobile": 24,
                "custom_spacing": true,
                "margin_top": 0,
                "margin_bottom": 0,
                "margin_left": 0,
                "margin_right": 0
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
            "text_WkHNYp": {
              "type": "text",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<ul><li>Dress in layers you can shed quickly, and keep the bedroom cool at night.</li><li>Notice your triggers. Spicy food, alcohol, caffeine and stress are common ones; a short symptom log makes patterns obvious.</li><li>Try slow, paced breathing at the first sign of a flash. Some studies find it reduces how bothersome flashes feel.</li></ul>",
                "text_style": "body",
                "alignment": "left",
                "text_color": "",
                "custom_font_size": false,
                "font_size": 16,
                "font_size_mobile": 15,
                "custom_line_height": true,
                "line_height": 36,
                "line_height_mobile": 24,
                "custom_spacing": true,
                "margin_top": 0,
                "margin_bottom": 0,
                "margin_left": 0,
                "margin_right": 0
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
            "text_V7eg8V": {
              "type": "text",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Products that may help.</strong> Some women find hormone-free supplements helpful, though the evidence is mixed and quality varies. We stock a small selection chosen for manufacturing quality, and we show the evidence level on every product page. Supplements are not FDA-approved to treat any condition, and what works for one woman may do nothing for another.</p>",
                "text_style": "body",
                "alignment": "left",
                "text_color": "",
                "custom_font_size": false,
                "font_size": 16,
                "font_size_mobile": 15,
                "custom_line_height": false,
                "line_height": 26,
                "line_height_mobile": 24,
                "custom_spacing": true,
                "margin_top": 0,
                "margin_bottom": 20,
                "margin_left": 0,
                "margin_right": 0
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
            "text_rUNRMn": {
              "type": "text",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<p><strong>When to see a clinician.</strong> If hot flashes are disrupting your sleep or your days, it is worth talking to a menopause-informed clinician. Effective treatments exist, including prescription options, and a clinician can help weigh benefits and risks for your situation. Bacton does not prescribe; we can help you prepare for that conversation.</p>",
                "text_style": "body",
                "alignment": "left",
                "text_color": "",
                "custom_font_size": false,
                "font_size": 16,
                "font_size_mobile": 15,
                "custom_line_height": false,
                "line_height": 26,
                "line_height_mobile": 24,
                "custom_spacing": false,
                "margin_top": 0,
                "margin_bottom": 16,
                "margin_left": 0,
                "margin_right": 0
              },
              "blocks": {}
            }
          },
          "block_order": [
            "heading_D8fypD",
